# ---- The gathering howl (cast-and-talk 3.21), story-heard: the dusk hour counts the wolves who reached it that hour
node nh_chorus
say Then lift your head and follow mine. Not the burying one. The other.
say[self.id=tn_cw_fallow_lakewick self.from=coldwater] Low first, then up like a rising fish, eh?
say[self.id=tn_cw_myrtle_stillford self.from=coldwater] Low first, then up like a rising fish, eh? That's how the loom master sings it.
say[self.id=tn_mb_aspen_marshholm self.from=larkfield] Up at the end, like a bid. That's it.
say[self.id=tn_sm_dun_harvestley] Up at the end, the way the market crier ends a price. That's it.
say[self.id=tn_sm_iris_meadowfield self.from=southmeadow] Round and warm, like an apple in the hand.
say[self.id=tn_sm_rusk_plummeadow self.from=southmeadow] Round and warm, like an apple in the hand. Ripe, that is.
say[self.id=tn_mb_roan_pikewater self.from=fenshore] Wet and low, then let it carry over the water.
say[self.id=cty_jory_corncroft self.from=fenshore] Wet and low, then let it carry over the water, no?
say[self.id=tn_fh_thistle_stonepick self.from=saltwell] Begin when the water is still. Saltwell begins nothing in a hurry.
say[self.id=cty_lavinia_mudworth self.from=saltwell] Begin when the water is still. Saltwell begins nothing in a hurry, honoured guest.
say[self.id=cty_ulla_rusthand self.from=oakhaven] Start low. Don't rush in. A sapling that rushes falls, mm?
say[self.id=tn_oh_cairn_oakhaven] Start low. Don't rush in. A sapling that rushes falls.
say[self.id=cty_ulric_stonevein self.from=frosthollow] Lower. Lower than that, cousin. There.
say[self.id=tn_fh_aster_coldfell self.from=frosthollow] Lower. Lower than that, cousin. There.
say[self.id=tn_mb_skye_eelbank self.from=fenshore] Twice round the reeds and back, no?
say[self.id=tn_mb_tamsin_eelbank self.from=fenshore] Twice round the reeds and back. Quietly, near the water.
say[self.id=rm_imogen_nailridge] I can't sing it, only write it. Sing it to me, and I'll get it down.
mood pleased
choice I was wondering about something else... -> hub
choice Goodbye for now. -> bye

# ---- The Names on the Water (Ch 5), in the bereaved's own window (the story counts one name a resident a wolf, from
# the 56 the campaign marks self.mem.nh_kin=wharf). The names themselves are never spoken: each answer gives a trade.
node nh_wn
say He was a crane hand. Forty years on the hoist and one bad rope. | He rowed the lighters. Nobody rowed them better. | He mended nets on the Mole. The tide took him off it. | A stevedore. Grayrock paid for the burying and nothing else.
say[self.id=rm_bettany_nailley] A crane hand. A Grayrock crane. Tell the priest that, and tell her I'll be there when she reads it.
say[self.id=rm_ingrid_ashwell] He loaded the lighters off the Mole. I'd have printed his name a hundred times if anyone had asked.
say[self.id=rm_rune_fogworth] A rope hand on the hoists. Quiet, like me. Say it slowly when it's read.
say[self.id=rm_ivar_pitchwick] He counted bales on the Grayrock quay. The Counting House sent a clerk to the burying. Me.
say[self.id=rm_bertil_grantham] He could whistle louder than the Bay Light. That's his name, really. Tell her that.
mood sad
choice I was wondering about something else... -> hub
choice Goodbye for now. -> bye
